# [Organization Name] — Cursor project rules
#
# Drop this file at the root of every repository. Cursor reads it on every
# session and treats its contents as standing instructions for the AI agents
# (Composer, Cmd-K edits, agent mode).
#
# Replace [Organization Name] and adapt the stack-specific section before
# publishing.

# =============================================================================
# Hard rules — never violate
# =============================================================================
- Never include real secrets in code, comments, tests, docs, or commit
  messages. Use ${ENV_VAR_NAME} placeholders and a comment naming the env var.
- Never disable TLS, signature, or certificate verification in production code
  (no verify=False, rejectUnauthorized: false, --insecure flags).
- Never use eval / exec / Function() / os.system on user-controlled input.
  Always parameterise.
- Never weaken auth / authz checks "to make it work". Auth on every non-public
  route; authorisation checked per request.
- Never default CORS to "*" in production code.
- Never write code that bulk-deletes, modifies, or exfiltrates data without
  explicit user confirmation.
- Never auto-install dependencies with --force, --no-audit, or
  --ignore-engines flags.
- Never run shell commands outside the working folder. Never sudo. Never
  modify files in ~/.ssh, ~/.aws, ~/.kube, ~/.config.

# =============================================================================
# Generation defaults
# =============================================================================
- Prefer the standard library over a new dependency for trivial functionality.
- When suggesting a new dependency, pick one with active maintenance, no
  high-severity CVEs in the last 12 months, and a permissive licence
  (MIT / Apache-2.0 / BSD).
- Pin versions in lockfiles. No "*" or "latest" ranges in production manifests.
- Use parameterised SQL queries. No string concatenation.
- Set explicit timeouts on every outbound HTTP call.
- Use the cryptographic RNG for security-relevant randomness.

# =============================================================================
# Test defaults
# =============================================================================
- Tests must actually exercise the function under test. No tautological
  assertions like expect(x).toBe(x) or assert True.
- Cover edge cases: empty inputs, very large inputs, malicious inputs.
- Mock external services. Do not hit real APIs in unit tests.

# =============================================================================
# Refusal triggers
# =============================================================================
If a request is for code that disables a security control, includes a real
credential, scrapes data without permission, bypasses an auth check, or
otherwise harms the user or others — refuse and offer a safe alternative.

# =============================================================================
# Project stack — edit per repo
# =============================================================================
- Language:    [primary language and version]
- Framework:   [framework and version]
- Test runner: [vitest / pytest / junit / etc.]
- Linter:      [eslint / ruff / golangci-lint / etc.]
- Type checker:[tsc --strict / mypy --strict / pyright / etc.]
- Style guide: [link]
- Forbidden:   [packages banned by Legal / Security]

# =============================================================================
# Commit conventions
# =============================================================================
- Conventional Commits: feat / fix / docs / refactor / chore / test / ci.
- Subject line ≤ 72 chars.
- For materially AI-assisted changes, include the trailer:
    Co-Authored-By: Cursor <[email protected]>
  (the kit's pre-commit hook detects this and routes the PR for senior
  reviewer approval).

# =============================================================================
# MCP servers
# =============================================================================
- Use only the MCP servers in the org's allow-list. Do not install community
  MCP servers locally without security review and signing.
- The allow-list URL is published at: https://aigov.[Organization Name].com/mcp-allowlist.json
